<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=talking-drum">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Talking drum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Dùndún, Tama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The talking drum is an hourglass-shaped drum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> with two drumheads joined by leather tension cords. Held under the arm, it is squeezed to change its pitch, so that a player can imitate the tone and rhythm of speech, though not its vowels or consonants. Europeans noticed its use as a means of communication in the first half of the 18th century, when messages could pass from village to village faster than a rider on horseback. Messages carried 4–5 miles, and relays of drummers using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> dùndún or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> atumpan could send news up to 20 miles. In the 20th century it entered popular music, including Mbalax, Fuji and Jùjú.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talking-drum.jpg" alt="Talking drum" width="330" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TalkingDrum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alongside Ifa, Yoruba religion is carried by oriki, praise poems that celebrate the deeds and character of each orisha, ancestor and lineage; by songs, chants and proverbs; and by the <b>talking drums</b>, which can reproduce the tones of the Yoruba language and speak praises and prayers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by the mid-18th century</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>West Africa</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Hourglass-shaped talking drums are said to be among the oldest instruments of West African griots and are traced back to the Bono, Yoruba, Hausa and the Ghana Empire, but no date is given for that earlier history; the earliest dated evidence is the notice taken by Europeans in the first half of the 18th century.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=talking-drum">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
